<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iwakura Mission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iwakura Mission was a Japanese embassy that toured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from 1871 to 1873, led by Iwakura Tomomi with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi">Kido Takayoshi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a>. It failed to revise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a>, but it studied Western government, industry, education and military systems, and its findings shaped the modernising reforms of the Meiji government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission.jpg" alt="Iwakura Mission" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iwakura_mission.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> had sent missions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1860 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1862 and 1863. The new Meiji government built on them with the Iwakura Mission. Its aim was threefold: to gain recognition for the newly reinstated imperial dynasty under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a>, to begin preliminary renegotiation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> with the dominant powers, and to make a comprehensive study of the industrial, political, military and educational systems of the United States and Europe. The Dutch-American missionary Guido Verbeck, an adviser to the government, first advocated it, submitting a proposal to Ōkuma Shigenobu in 1869 and discussing it with Iwakura Tomomi in 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Iwakura Tomomi was ambassador extraordinary and plenipotentiary, and his four vice-ambassadors were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi">Kido Takayoshi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> and Yamaguchi Naoyoshi. Kume Kunitake served as secretary and chronicler, and his account appeared in 1878 in five volumes. About 100 people sailed from Yokohama on 23 December 1871 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> on 15 January 1872, then crossed by train to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, on 29 February. The stay was extended by an attempt to negotiate new treaty rights, which failed, and the party sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in August 1872. Several students were left to study abroad, among them five young women, including Tsuda Umeko, who later founded the school that became Tsuda University.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, from 17 August 1872, the mission inspected political, academic and military institutions and industrial centres, among them Manchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a>, Sheffield and Saltaire. It had an audience with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> at Windsor Castle on 5 December. In 1873 it visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and it reached Yokohama again on 13 September 1873.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The mission did not achieve the revision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a>, and the attempt prolonged it by almost four months and drew criticism that its members had gone beyond their mandate. Its members were nonetheless favourably impressed by the industrial modernisation they saw, and the tour gave them a strong impetus to lead similar initiatives on their return. Makino Nobuaki, who went as a student, wrote in his memoirs that the mission, together with the abolition of the han system, was among the most important events that built the foundation of the state after the Restoration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iwakura Mission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government established model factories such as the Tomioka silk mill, subsidised strategic industries, and brought in technologies from Europe and the United States through systematic study missions, most famously the <b>Iwakura Mission</b> of 1871 to 1873.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
